{"name":"Sweet Perfume","hex":"#d49ab9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"perfume","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pleasant smell; the scent, odor, or odoriferous particles emitted from a sweet-smelling substance; a pleasant odor.","A substance created to provide a pleasant smell or one which emits an agreeable odor."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply perfume to; to fill or impregnate with a perfume; to scent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/perfume"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"},{"title":"Perfume","blocks":[{"text":"Perfume is a mixture of fragrant essential oils or aroma compounds (fragrances), fixatives and solvents, usually in liquid form, used to give the human body, animals, food, objects, and living-spaces an agreeable scent. Perfumes can be defined as substances that emit and diffuse a pleasant and fragrant odor. They consist of artificial mixtures of aromatic chemicals and essential oils. The 1939 Nobel Laureate for Chemistry, Leopold Ružička stated in 1945 that \"right from the earliest days of scientific chemistry up to the present time, perfumes have substantially contributed to the development of organic chemistry as regards methods, systematic classification, and theory.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perfume"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/1280px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boys Pilfering Molasses – On The Quays, New Orleans, 1853 painting by George Henry Hall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sweetness is perceived by the taste buds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray1018.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp